// Test cases, the lines that follow a function's return clause:
//
//   add(int a, int b) { int sum = a + b; } return int sum;
//   (1, 2):(3)
//   (0, 0):(0)[time:O(1)]
//
// See ../../../../../../../docs/TESTS.md. They are part of the declaration,
// not source: nothing here reaches the program's emitted C, which is why a
// program carrying cases compiles to exactly the same output with or without
// them. They reach the test harness instead (back/tgt/c/emit/prog/test/),
// which bin/idc builds and runs before the program on every build.
//
// There is no ambiguity with the next declaration -- a function starts
// `ident (`, a case starts `(` or the keyword `given` -- so this is a plain
// "while the next token opens a case" loop, run after the function has been
// pushed so each case can record which function it belongs to.

// Returns 0 so it can be called from push_func's return clause, which is the
// only free slot there under the action limit. The function each case belongs
// to is the last one pushed onto `prog`, which is why this runs after it.
take_cases(int[] pos) {
  while((cur_kind(pos) == "op" && cur_text(pos) == "(") || (cur_kind(pos) == "kw" && cur_text(pos) == "given")) {
    push_case(pos);
  }
} return int 0;

// A case is a "case" node -- its arguments in nl1, its expected values in nl2
// -- and it is also recorded as the half-open token range it occupies, so that
// two cases can be compared without rebuilding their text.
push_case(int[] pos) {
  int node = parse_case(pos);
  push((import casenode), node);
  case_owner();
} return void;

case_owner() {
  int len_v = len((import prog));
  push((import casefn), (import prog)[len_v - 1]);
} return void;
